      "math_academy_id": "q-87147",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/d3c459cda9cd4cf2c1cb6b0a84b66108fab84d59a87bd14ed11b6ea80cbf8212.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(4x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(4x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(4x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/2fe8a51d1b19bbe18f90b80692df31a01debd536a46569104b1e01a18f2bae87.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(4x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/d3865abcbb54d17f0af7594c7c50de9301e795b7ac1c364b180e782aa0cf94be.png)",
